1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,367
Two sisters.

2
00:00:01,367 --> 00:00:03,067
A shuffled pack of cards.

3
00:00:03,067 --> 00:00:04,381
A grain of pollen.

4
00:00:04,781 --> 00:00:07,880
Diya's two cousins have the
same mother and the same father.

5
00:00:07,880 --> 00:00:10,939
Yet one has curly hair, and
the other has straight hair.

6
00:00:11,389 --> 00:00:15,541
She wants to be a doctor, and she asked:
if they come from the same parents,

7
00:00:15,541 --> 00:00:16,923
why are they not alike?

8
00:00:17,273 --> 00:00:21,787
Part of the answer is a special division
that makes sperm, eggs and pollen.

9
00:00:21,787 --> 00:00:24,738
It halves the chromosomes,
and it shuffles them.

10
00:00:24,738 --> 00:00:26,146
It is called meiosis.

11
00:00:26,646 --> 00:00:31,082
By the end, you will walk through
meiosis one, stage by stage, including

12
00:00:31,082 --> 00:00:33,108
the five parts of prophase one.

13
00:00:33,758 --> 00:00:36,198
First, why do we need a special division?

14
00:00:36,548 --> 00:00:39,763
Your body cells have two
sets of chromosomes, two n.

15
00:00:39,763 --> 00:00:43,213
One set came from your mother,
and one from your father.

16
00:00:43,663 --> 00:00:48,193
If a sperm and an egg each had
two n, the baby would have four n.

17
00:00:48,193 --> 00:00:51,164
So each gamete must carry only one set, n.

18
00:00:51,614 --> 00:00:52,853
Meiosis does this.

19
00:00:52,853 --> 00:00:57,222
It has two divisions, meiosis one and
meiosis two, but the DNA is copied

20
00:00:57,222 --> 00:00:58,122
only once.

21
00:00:58,033 --> 00:01:00,669
Meiosis one is where the number is halved.

22
00:01:01,319 --> 00:01:04,848
Let's zoom in, to where
meiosis happens in a flower.

23
00:01:05,198 --> 00:01:06,513
Here is a flower.

24
00:01:06,513 --> 00:01:08,985
The pollen is made inside the anther.

25
00:01:09,485 --> 00:01:11,138
Zoom into the anther.

26
00:01:11,138 --> 00:01:15,325
It holds pollen mother cells,
cells that divide by meiosis.

27
00:01:15,825 --> 00:01:17,233
Zoom into one nucleus.

28
00:01:17,233 --> 00:01:21,370
The DNA is already copied, so each
chromosome has two sister chromatids.

29
00:01:21,870 --> 00:01:22,897
Look closely.

30
00:01:22,897 --> 00:01:27,084
The chromosomes come in look
alike pairs, one from each parent.

31
00:01:27,084 --> 00:01:29,239
These are homologous chromosomes.

32
00:01:29,239 --> 00:01:30,477
Homo means same.

33
00:01:31,127 --> 00:01:33,590
Prophase one is long and complex.

34
00:01:33,590 --> 00:01:35,105
It has five parts.

35
00:01:35,105 --> 00:01:36,445
Let's watch them.

36
00:01:36,795 --> 00:01:37,695
One.

37
00:01:37,299 --> 00:01:38,199
Leptotene.

38
00:01:38,099 --> 00:01:41,832
The chromosomes slowly become
visible, and keep condensing.

39
00:01:42,582 --> 00:01:43,482
Two.

40
00:01:43,031 --> 00:01:43,931
Zygotene.

41
00:01:43,699 --> 00:01:46,221
The homologous chromosomes
start to pair up.

42
00:01:46,221 --> 00:01:47,346
This is synapsis.

43
00:01:47,346 --> 00:01:50,810
A structure called the synaptonemal
complex forms between them.

44
00:01:50,810 --> 00:01:55,240
The pair is called a bivalent, or a
tetrad, because it has four chromatids.

45
00:01:55,990 --> 00:01:56,890
Three.

46
00:01:56,541 --> 00:01:57,441
Pachytene.

47
00:01:57,272 --> 00:01:58,969
Recombination nodules appear.

48
00:01:58,969 --> 00:02:01,416
Here, non sister chromatids swap pieces.

49
00:02:01,416 --> 00:02:04,982
This is crossing over, done by
an enzyme called recombinase.

50
00:02:05,732 --> 00:02:06,632
Four.

51
00:02:06,191 --> 00:02:07,091
Diplotene.

52
00:02:06,855 --> 00:02:10,674
The synaptonemal complex dissolves,
and the pair starts to separate, except

53
00:02:10,674 --> 00:02:12,059
where they crossed over.

54
00:02:12,059 --> 00:02:14,034
These X shaped points are chiasmata.

55
00:02:14,784 --> 00:02:15,684
Five.

56
00:02:15,303 --> 00:02:16,203
Diakinesis.

57
00:02:16,101 --> 00:02:18,336
The chiasmata slide toward the ends.

58
00:02:18,336 --> 00:02:19,848
This is terminalisation.

59
00:02:19,848 --> 00:02:24,216
The spindle forms, the nucleolus
disappears, and the nuclear envelope breaks

60
00:02:24,216 --> 00:02:25,116
down.

61
00:02:25,486 --> 00:02:27,433
Now the rest of meiosis one.

62
00:02:27,783 --> 00:02:28,744
Metaphase one.

63
00:02:28,744 --> 00:02:31,008
The bivalents line up at the equator.

64
00:02:31,008 --> 00:02:35,024
Fibres from opposite poles attach
to the two chromosomes of each pair.

65
00:02:35,524 --> 00:02:36,424
Anaphase one.

66
00:02:36,412 --> 00:02:39,971
The homologous chromosomes separate,
and move to opposite poles.

67
00:02:39,971 --> 00:02:43,801
But the sister chromatids stay
together, joined at their centromeres.

68
00:02:44,301 --> 00:02:45,371
Telophase one.

69
00:02:45,371 --> 00:02:50,134
The nuclear membrane and the nucleolus
reappear, and the cytoplasm divides.

70
00:02:50,134 --> 00:02:54,266
We get a dyad of cells, two
cells, each with one set, n.

71
00:02:54,766 --> 00:02:57,521
Then comes a short pause,
called interkinesis.

72
00:02:57,521 --> 00:02:59,280
The DNA is not copied here.

73
00:02:59,280 --> 00:03:00,797
Then meiosis two begins.

74
00:03:01,447 --> 00:03:05,028
Side by side, anaphase of
mitosis and anaphase one.

75
00:03:05,028 --> 00:03:06,194
What separates.

76
00:03:06,194 --> 00:03:08,369
In mitosis, sister chromatids.

77
00:03:08,369 --> 00:03:11,127
In anaphase one, homologous chromosomes.

78
00:03:11,877 --> 00:03:12,777
Centromeres.

79
00:03:12,649 --> 00:03:14,125
In mitosis, they split.

80
00:03:14,125 --> 00:03:17,788
In anaphase one, they do not
split, so the sisters stay joined.

81
00:03:18,538 --> 00:03:19,438
Result.

82
00:03:19,134 --> 00:03:20,478
Mitosis keeps two n.

83
00:03:20,478 --> 00:03:23,041
Meiosis one halves it, from two n to n.

84
00:03:23,041 --> 00:03:26,470
That is why meiosis one is
called the reductional division.

85
00:03:27,220 --> 00:03:28,120
Your turn.

86
00:03:27,997 --> 00:03:32,159
You see homologous chromosomes pairing
up, and a synaptonemal complex forming

87
00:03:32,159 --> 00:03:33,069
between them.

88
00:03:33,069 --> 00:03:34,766
Which part of prophase one?

89
00:03:34,766 --> 00:03:35,909
Pause and decide.

90
00:03:39,109 --> 00:03:40,009
Zygotene!

91
00:03:39,891 --> 00:03:43,358
The pairing, called synapsis,
happens in zygotene.

92
00:03:44,008 --> 00:03:45,099
Remember this.

93
00:03:45,099 --> 00:03:46,556
Pairing in zygotene.

94
00:03:46,556 --> 00:03:48,432
Crossing over in pachytene.

95
00:03:48,432 --> 00:03:50,043
Chiasmata in diplotene.

96
00:03:50,643 --> 00:03:52,260
Now, a worked example.

97
00:03:52,260 --> 00:03:53,160
Six events.

98
00:03:53,140 --> 00:03:55,005
Prophase one, or after it?

99
00:03:55,356 --> 00:03:57,147
The synaptonemal complex forms.

100
00:03:57,147 --> 00:03:58,806
Prophase one, in zygotene.

101
00:03:59,256 --> 00:04:02,348
Homologous chromosomes
move to opposite poles.

102
00:04:02,348 --> 00:04:04,196
After, in anaphase one.

103
00:04:04,646 --> 00:04:05,802
Crossing over.

104
00:04:05,802 --> 00:04:07,897
Prophase one, in pachytene.

105
00:04:08,347 --> 00:04:10,273
Bivalents line up at the equator.

106
00:04:10,273 --> 00:04:11,799
After, in metaphase one.

107
00:04:12,249 --> 00:04:14,005
Terminalisation of chiasmata.

108
00:04:14,005 --> 00:04:15,864
Prophase one, in diakinesis.

109
00:04:16,314 --> 00:04:18,296
And a dyad of cells forms.

110
00:04:18,296 --> 00:04:20,153
After, in telophase one.

111
00:04:20,803 --> 00:04:22,267
Now, the exam trap.

112
00:04:22,617 --> 00:04:26,512
Many students think crossing over
happens in diplotene, because that is

113
00:04:26,512 --> 00:04:28,298
where the chiasmata are seen.

114
00:04:28,698 --> 00:04:33,145
But crossing over happens in pachytene,
at the recombination nodules.

115
00:04:33,145 --> 00:04:37,713
In diplotene, the pairs pull apart,
and the chiasmata simply show where

116
00:04:37,713 --> 00:04:38,694
it happened.

117
00:04:39,344 --> 00:04:40,707
Where is this asked?

118
00:04:40,707 --> 00:04:45,033
As the order of the five substages,
matching each substage with its event,

119
00:04:45,033 --> 00:04:47,191
and what separates in anaphase one.

120
00:04:47,791 --> 00:04:48,968
Exam level now.

121
00:04:48,968 --> 00:04:50,294
Read the question.

122
00:04:50,294 --> 00:04:51,521
Pause and solve.

123
00:04:56,921 --> 00:04:58,303
The answer is D.

124
00:04:58,803 --> 00:05:03,716
Crossing over happens at recombination
nodules in pachytene, done by the

125
00:05:03,716 --> 00:05:05,187
enzyme recombinase.

126
00:05:05,687 --> 00:05:09,826
Zygotene is the pairing, and
diplotene only shows the chiasmata.

127
00:05:10,426 --> 00:05:11,966
Here is your recap.

128
00:05:11,966 --> 00:05:13,549
Screenshot this card.

129
00:05:16,749 --> 00:05:18,935
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

130
00:05:19,335 --> 00:05:20,235
One.

131
00:05:19,787 --> 00:05:22,791
Name the five substages
of prophase one, in order.

132
00:05:23,691 --> 00:05:24,591
Two.

133
00:05:24,133 --> 00:05:25,380
What is a bivalent?

134
00:05:26,280 --> 00:05:27,180
Three.

135
00:05:26,840 --> 00:05:28,768
What separates in anaphase one?

136
00:05:29,868 --> 00:05:34,871
Next time: if meiosis one already halved
the chromosomes, why divide again?

137
00:05:34,871 --> 00:05:38,795
We find out in meiosis two and
the significance of meiosis.
